      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Two hundred and eight patients of low back pain were studied in the department of orthopaedic surgery, GSVM Medical College, Kanpur. The patients were put on intensive conservative treatment in the form of analgesics, hard bed rest, spinal extension exercises, traction and lumbosacral support. Thirty-two patients did not respond to this treatment and their symptoms were of more than 6 months duration and they were subjected to epidural injection of local anaesthetic agent, saline and corticosteroids. The failed 6 cases were investigated for consideration of surgery. The aim of this study is to develop standardised strategy for the treatment of low back pain. For the low back pain patients not responding to intensive conservative treatment, an idea has been put forward in the form of epidural medication prior to considering them to surgery to prevent unnecessary incidence of \"failed back\". A trial of epidural injection is suggested to avoid surgery."},
